rself  turning to food to shield your emotions  on a regular basis it's probably  something that we need to address we  want to learn to be present with our  food not use it as a distraction from  our life next to sedation things become  concerning when we start to use food as  a way to numb our feelings some people  like to call this a food coma because it  involves eating to the point where you  don't notice your feelings your hunger  or really anything anymore you're  basically on autopilot this type of  eating is very common in people who feel  like their lives are attica  troll and use food as a way to  completely mute what's going on in  severe cases this can lead to social  isolation missing work or feeling  completely hopeless about life hunger  and satiety cues are usually completely  ignored or not noticed and this can lead  to spiraling out of control with our  overeating next is punishment now for  those of you who use food as a form of  sedation this can lead to self blame and  punishing behaviors in severe cases some  people may overeat to the point where  they feel super sick in order to punish  themselves this severe form of emotional  eating can be detrimental to self esteem  and result in complete self-hatred  typically those who punish themselves  with food report no enjoyment from food  whatsoever and in fact say that they  hate food for this type of severe eating  compassion and understanding is really  important which comes from the nurturer  voice that I talked about in principle  number four moreover seeking  professional help from a psychologist  and a dietician may also be really  helpful okay now that we have an  understanding of the ways people cope  with food let's dive into why people  emotionally eat a lot of people are not  even aware that they're eating  emotionally and just kind of brush it  off as well it tastes good  but as an intuitive eater if you openly  accept all foods then they're always  available this means that even if the  food tastes good you can stop at any  time because it'll be available the next  time that you want it chances are that  it'll actually taste even better when  you're actually hungry for it if you're  still eating after you're full I want  you to ask yourself if my body feels  satisfied after eating a certain amount  of food but I still continue to eat  after I'm full  what other need am I trying to resolve  with this food you may realize that  you're using food to address some other  major feelings so let's go over some of  the major ones first is boredom and  procrastination this is probably one of  the most common reasons for eating so  for example you might be watching your  daily soaps and  turn to a snack or maybe you're waiting  for a friend to kind of pick you up and  you're snacking on candy while you wait  in these cases you're not eating because  you're hungry but rather because you're  entertaining yourself with food next is  bribery and reward throw out our lives  we've probably used food as a reward or  even as a bribe to get somebody to do  something for example you may tell your  kids that they can pick out a chocolate  bar if they behave while your shop or  maybe you reward yourself with a  doughnut for doing a really hard workout  while there is nothing wrong with  enjoying foods in good times using food  as a reward can actually be  self-perpetuating there are always going  to be things in your life to reward  yourself for and there will always be  times when your kids need to behave  trust me  using food as the solution creates an  expectation that food will always be the  reward or bribe for certain good  behaviors next is excitement one of the  reasons that people like to go on diets  is because they can trigger feelings of  excitement and hope this diet is going  to be the one it's that newness that  really excites people and motivates them  to get started  doing something that let's be real they  don't actually really want to do over  time we do tend to get bored of the same  old food same old life and want to just  inherently mix things up problem is  using food as a way to add excitement to  your life usually leads to feelings of  despair when that diet stops working  that excitement turns to upset and leads  you to use food again in order to cope  then you try a new diet it's exciting at  first stops working you cope as you can  see this creates a bit of a cycle that  is really hard to break next is soothing  so using candy as a way to add sweetness  to your life is a temporary fix to a  much larger problem although food of  course can bring out really good  feelings if you're finding yourself  constantly using eating to incite  relaxation or happiness this can involve  into a poor relationship with food  next is love anger and stress food can  be used as a way to express love so for  example making a meal for your partner  can be a way to show them that you  appreciate them and that you care about  them it can also be a way to express  anger so for example turning to really  crunchy chips to physically chomp down  on something is commonly used as a way  to relieve anger or just to annoy your  husband like I often do anyways finally  food can be used or not used to deal  with stress during acute stress there is  a rush of adrenaline known as the  fight-or-flight response which was  designed to help us run away from  threats this occurs more with acute  stress meaning that in Stemper Airy  problem is the stress of the experience  tends to be chronic stress which raises  our cortisol levels and can lead to  extra storage of abdominal fat if you  compound this with stress eating this  often leads people into overeating and  to the idea of blowing your diet which  in itself causes stress that perpetuates  the cycle next is a sense of connection  whether you belong to a religious group  sports team or other organization  feeling a part of a group is a really  important part of human survival if  going in for wings after a game is a  routine for your sports team I mean you  may feel inclined to eat even if you're  not really hungry instead of feeling  like a diet Debbie that everyone is  gonna nag you may just eat anyway and  feel dissatisfied or just like really  uncomfortably full this is really common  for people who want to bond with others  but struggle to find a way to manage  social eating situations and their  actual unique needs alright so now that  we have an idea of how and why people  emotionally eat  how can you cope with your emotions  without using food well the first thing  that I want you to try is to ask  yourself four important questions in a  four step process am I actually hungry  if yes then my all means honor your  hunger and eat if not then answer the  next question what am I feeling if  you're not biologically hungry then what  are you hungry for what are you feeling  for most people this isn't something  that's really easy to pinpoint so I  would suggest trying one of the  following use a journal and write out  your feelings and emotions chat with a  friend to vent sit in a quiet room and  let yourself experience these feelings  you might be really surprised how much  this can help or seek out some  professional help from a counselor or  psychologist once you've determined this  you can move on to the next question and  that is what do I need in a lot of cases  people eat to fulfill an unmet need so  for example you might find yourself  turning to candy and sweets after work  every day even if you're not actually  hungry after diving into how you're  feeling you might notice that you feel  burnt out and really tired instead of  food what you likely need is some rest  and sleep so to solve this problem  focusing on sleep hygiene may be a good  first step to do this it's time to move  to step 4 and that may mean asking for a  little bit of support if you notice that  you're really burnt out finding time for  yourself may be really hard to do but  it's so essential to breaking the cycle  of emotional eating  whether it's your boss or your partner  asking for time for yourself may be  really helpful for example ask your boss  for a personal day or ask your partner  to watch the kids for 45 minutes while  you take a bath or go to a yoga class  uninterrupted time to yourself may help  meet your actual needs for relaxation  and also help you realize that food must  not the solution to your problem in the  first place there are many ways that  people can meet their needs without food  and this will ultimately be very  different for each individual person  with that being said there are some  basic needs that almost everyone needs  that do not involve food and that is  rest sensual pleasure ability to express  one's self and feelings be heard  understood and accepted being stimulated  both intellectually and creatively and  comfort and warmth  if these needs are not met you may turn  to food in order to cope and while it  may be easy for some it can be really  hard for a lot of other people just to  meet these basic needs Evelyn Tribble  Ian  should give some really great  suggestions for dealing with your  feelings in their book one write in a  journal and express your true feelings  to talk to a friend on the phone or in  person if you're like me you may want to  maybe talk to a few record yourself on  your phone this man should be really  helpful if no one is available at the  moment so that you can have a bit of a  conversation with yourself to kind of  hash things out for physically express  your feelings in a safe way like maybe  punching a bag or a pillow confront the  person who is upsetting you if you're  feeling safe to do so  cry as much as you want I mean it is  very natural and it is a healthy way to  deal with grief anger sadness etc sit  and let yourself discover your feelings  and emotions seek out professional help  this is actually not a last resort and  it's actually something that I recommend  everybody try to access if they can and  finally engage in some acts of self-care  so some popular acts of self-care that  you can utilize in your journey include  meditation reading a book relaxing on  the couch taking a sauna or a hot tub if  you can listening to music playing games  with friends family or a pet taking a  relaxing bath getting a massage going  for a walk or some other form of  physical activity and asking someone for  a hug the human hug is one of the most  profound but helpful ways to cope it  actually releases a cascade of feel-good  chemicals that can be calming relaxing  and provide support if you don't have  someone to hug at the moment even  hugging a stuffed animal or a real  animal can be really helpful my personal  outlet is exercise I know such a  dietician thing to say but I'm not  talking about doing like a CrossFit  session or sprinting on the treadmill  for 90 minutes no thank you  even just going for a walk with my  toddler or my husband can absolutely  help research has found that exercise  and other healthy coping mechanisms can  be a really effective way to manage  stress and emotional eating so exercise  in particular provides a healthy  distraction from your stress while also  providing a boost of endorphins and an  outlet  that can ultimately help to relieve  stress as you navigate this principle I  want to emphasize that you may deal with  some discomfort for a while after a  lifetime of using food to cope with your  emotions it may be really overwhelming  to experience your emotions and your  feelings up close it's actually  completely okay to enjoy foods flavors  and the feelings that we get from it  this is healthy but we really want to  get away from using these experiences to  fill a void or cover up underlying  problems that you're facing as I've said  throughout the series intuitive eating  is not a straight road you are going to  have times when you eat emotionally and  that is perfectly fine the goal is to  identify emotional eating and navigate  the reason behind it  in some ways having this setback may  actually help you realize how far you've  really come you may notice that you find  it easier to identify the reasons for  your emotional eating and find  appropriate outlets it may also be a  clue  that you need to reassess your current  life situation if you're eating because  you're angry look into what is causing  that anger if you're eating because  you're tired or bored what can you do to  solve that by being a food  anthropologist you can start to identify  trends in your behavior and begin to  connect them to situations in your real  life so instead of feeling out of  control appreciate that you've developed  the skills to do this and identify the  problem early before it gets completely  out of control as you continue through  your intuitive eating journey remember  that food is something that we should  all enjoy and use to nourish our body  and our mind your relationship with food  will become so much stronger when you  use food for a pleasurable experience  rather than just as a way to cope so for  this week I want you to identify the  reasons for your emotional eating using  the four steps or questions can be  really super helpful am I actually  hungry what am I feeling what do I need  and can I have some support in addition  to this I wanted to find at least one  non-food coping mechanism that  feels good to you so for example see if  exercise helps to relieve your stress or  maybe you like dealing with stress by  reading a book whatever it is find  something that you genuinely find  relaxing if you reach for food try doing  this first if you're still wanting food  after this then assess whether you're  eating emotionally or if you're actually  hungry again this may take time to learn  how to do but over time you will have a  much easier experience identifying the  reasons behind your food choices I want  to of course a flag that it is  absolutely okay to be eating for  emotional reasons the key is to identify  these reasons to be accepting of them to  be compassionate with yourself and to  move on and to not let those food  choices inform the rest of your day the  rest of your week the rest of your month  and your general effect so continue to  work on all of these steps throughout  your intuitive eating journey and stay  tuned for our next video on principle  number 8 respect your body alright folks  that's all for today don't forget to  give this video the thumbs up leave me a  comment below if you have any questions  about intuitive eating subscribe to the  channel and I'll see you next time on  Abby's kitchen bye  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

